sections.body:
Generic code never hard-codes a type it can ask for:

* `zero(T)`, `one(T)` (or `zero(x)`): the additive and multiplicative
  identities of a type, for initialising sums and products;
* `eltype(v)`: the element type of a collection;
* `similar(v)`, `similar(v, T)`, `similar(v, T, dims)`: a new uninitialised
  array "like" `v` (a range or a view gives a `Vector`);
* `promote_type(S, T)`: the type that a mixed operation produces, e.g. the
  type of an accumulator over two vectors of different element types.

In the first example notice the last line: the empty inputs return a zero of
the right type, which a literal `0` could not do.

